5. {'MESH:D009396': {'cui': 'MESH:D009396', 'name': 'Wilms Tumor', 'types': 'Disease', 'aliases': "Bilateral Wilms Tumor|Nephroblastoma|Nephroblastomas|Tumor, Bilateral Wilms|Tumor, Wilms|Tumor, Wilms'|Wilm's Tumor|Wilms' Tumor|Wilms Tumor 1|Wilms Tumor, Bilateral|Wilm Tumor|WT1", 'definition': "A malignant kidney tumor, caused by the uncontrolled multiplication of renal stem (blastemal), stromal (STROMAL CELLS), and epithelial (EPITHELIAL CELLS) elements. However, not all three are present in every case. Several genes or chromosomal areas have been associated with Wilms tumor which is usually found in childhood as a firm lump in a child's side or ABDOMEN."}
6. {'MESH:D005909': {'cui': 'MESH:D005909', 'name': 'Glioblastoma', 'types': 'Disease', 'aliases': 'Astrocytoma, Grade IV|Astrocytomas, Grade IV|Giant Cell Glioblastoma|Giant Cell Glioblastomas|Glioblastoma, Giant Cell|Glioblastoma Multiforme|Glioblastomas|Glioblastomas, Giant Cell|Grade IV Astrocytoma|Grade IV Astrocytomas', 'definition': 'A malignant form of astrocytoma histologically characterized by pleomorphism of cells, nuclear atypia, microhemorrhage, and necrosis. They may arise in any region of the central nervous system, with a predilection for the cerebral hemispheres, basal ganglia, and commissural pathways. Clinical presentation most frequently occurs in the fifth or sixth decade of life with focal neurologic signs or seizures.'}
